#!/bin/sh
# my/daily is a rebuild target, not a branch: `./stack daily` recreates it with
# `git switch -C my/daily upstream/main`, so a commit made here is discarded on the next
# rebuild and can never reach a PR. Its companion is pre-merge-commit, which git invokes
# instead of this hook when a merge resolves cleanly.
#
# There is deliberately no "a merge is in progress, so this must be the rebuild" exemption.
# Merge state is not evidence of origin: a refused hand merge leaves MERGE_HEAD behind, and
# aborting a conflicted rebuild and re-merging the same leaf by hand reproduces the same sha.
# The rebuild's own commits pass because it exports STACK_DAILY; a conflict it hands you is
# finished with `./stack daily --continue`, which sets that too.

branch=$(git symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD) || exit 0

# The main checkout is the integration tree. Every development branch must commit from its own
# linked worktree, which `stack new` and `stack wt` create.
gitdir=$(cd "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)" && pwd -P)
common=$(cd "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)" && pwd -P)
if [ "$branch" != "my/daily" ] && [ "$gitdir" = "$common" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

stack: refusing to commit $branch from the main checkout.

Development branches commit from their own linked worktrees. Keep the main checkout on
my/daily. Preserve these edits, switch the main checkout back, then run:

    ./stack wt $branch

See CLAUDE.md, "Start here", for moving uncommitted edits without losing them.

EOF
    exit 1
fi

[ "$branch" = "my/daily" ] || exit 0
[ -n "$STACK_DAILY" ] && exit 0

if [ -e "$(git rev-parse --git-path MERGE_HEAD)" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'

stack: refusing to commit a merge on my/daily by hand.

If this is the conflict ./stack daily stopped on: resolve it, `git add -u` (never `-A`), then

    ./stack daily --continue        # commits this merge and finishes the remaining leaves

Anything else: `git merge --abort`, then `./stack daily`. my/daily is reconstructed from the
ledger, never advanced by hand - that is what keeps merge order and the rerere resolutions
meaningful.

EOF
    exit 1
fi

cat >&2 <<'EOF'

stack: refusing a non-merge commit on my/daily.

my/daily is the integration tree, rebuilt from upstream/main by `./stack daily`. It holds
merge commits only. A commit made here is thrown away by the next rebuild and belongs to no
branch, so it can never be pushed or reviewed.

Move the work to its own branch instead - nothing is lost, the edits stay on disk:

    git stash create                 # prints a sha; nothing in the tree changes
    ./stack new fix/<name>           # worktree at ../claudecodeui.<name>, cut from upstream/main
    cd ../claudecodeui.<name> && git checkout <sha> -- <the files you own>

Never `git reset --hard` or `git checkout -- .` to get past this - that is the one action
that does destroy the work. See CLAUDE.md, "Start here".

EOF
exit 1
